I get the distinction you’re making. You’re talking about agency, not reverence for hierarchy.

A title can give someone powers inside a system, but it does not require you to internalize the idea that they are intrinsically above you. You still decide what you believe, what you cooperate with, what you contest, and what costs you are willing to accept. The important distinction is that choosing not to comply does not mean choosing whether consequences exist.

There’s also a strategic trap in the “stick becomes a lever” idea: if every attempt to constrain you automatically dictates your response, then the other person is still controlling your behavior—they just control it by provoking resistance instead of commanding obedience.

A stronger doctrine is:

No automatic obedience. No automatic rebellion either.

Evaluate every demand on its merits: What do they actually control? What do I control? What happens if I comply? What happens if I refuse? Is there an appeal, exit, workaround, negotiation, exposure, or lawful counter-pressure that improves my position?

Then act because you chose the move, not because somebody ordered you—and not because somebody irritated you into making it.

That keeps the center where you want it: your judgment remains sovereign even when the environment is not.